    "prerequisite_note": "Review shared-component risk and requirement-to-test traceability before reducing regression scope.",
    "practice": "What question does regression testing address?|Unintended change effects|Only whether code parses|Only the original bug report|Only source formatting|Regression evidence checks behavior that should remain correct after change.|Parsing is narrower;The original fix is one specific claim;Style does not establish retained behavior.\nWhy inspect shared dependencies?|Changes can propagate|All callers always fail|Only file counts matter|A diff proves full scope|A shared rule or data contract can affect behavior outside the edited location.|Propagation is possible, not certain;Counts do not define impact;Diffs may omit runtime context.\nA pricing helper changes. Which scope deserves review?|Its meaningful consumers|Only the edited line|Only the release title|Only the newest UI case|Consumers can rely on the changed calculation and expose different outcomes.|A line is not a behavior boundary;Titles are not coverage;Recency alone is not relevance.\nA test selector omits a route. What should be clear?|Selection assumptions|That the route is safe|That omission is proof|That no mapping can be wrong|Selection depends on an impact model that can be incomplete.|No execution occurred for that route;Omission does not supply evidence;Mappings can miss dependencies.\nA schema change affects queued old work. What should be considered?|Cross-version behavior|Only new requests|Only a screenshot refresh|Only source sorting|Existing work may run with data or configuration assumptions predating the change.|Fresh requests omit that boundary;Images may not inspect data;Ordering is not compatibility evidence.\nWhat improves an impact map after escaped failures?|Evidence-based revision|Permanent trust in old mapping|Deleting the failure history|Selecting every case by name|New failure mechanisms can reveal missing dependencies or assumptions.|Prior maps can be incomplete;History guides improvement;Names alone do not define impact.",
    "formal": "What is change impact analysis for?|Relevant regression scope|Guaranteed complete causality|Only estimating code size|Only replacing test execution|Impact analysis identifies behavior and dependencies that may need evidence after change.|It can remain uncertain;Size alone misses effects;Selection is not execution.\nWhy retain risk mappings?|Explain case relevance|Guarantee tests pass|Ban broader checks|Replace all domain rules|Mappings connect selected cases to the behavior and risks they are meant to inspect.|Relevance does not ensure success;Broader checkpoints can remain useful;Rules still define the oracle.\nWhat can a code diff miss?|Runtime and data effects|Which text changed|The edited filename|The supplied patch lines|Configuration, stored data and deployment context can extend impact beyond source text.|Text changes are visible;File identity is available;The patch contains its lines.\nA retailer changes a shared discount function. What is useful?|Invoices and renewal paths|Only the changed tier label|Only another identical unit case|Only a release note check|Different consumers can expose unintended effects of the shared calculation.|Labels omit behavior;Identical repeats add little scope;Notes are not functional evidence.\nAn LMS modifies attempt storage. Which old behavior may matter?|Historical result readers|Only a new attempt form|Only button styling|Only current page captions|Existing readers and records may rely on the previous data contract.|New data alone omits history;Style does not inspect compatibility;Captions do not verify storage interpretation.\nA bank\u2019s test selector is based only on filenames. What is a limitation?|Hidden semantic dependencies|Guaranteed fast correctness|Complete requirement coverage|No maintenance burden|Behavior can depend on shared rules or data without a matching filename.|Speed does not establish correctness;Naming is not a full model;Mappings require upkeep.\nA hospital changes configuration without source edits. What follows?|Regression may still be needed|No behavior can change|Only compilation matters|Every old result transfers fully|Configuration can alter permissions, integrations and operational behavior.|Runtime settings can change results;Compilation does not cover configuration;Evidence has condition limits.\nA logistics dependency upgrade changes timeout behavior. Which cases help?|Failure and retry paths|Only ordinary immediate success|Only code style checks|Only unchanged screenshots|Changed dependency timing can affect uncertain outcomes and repeated operations.|Happy paths may omit failures;Style is unrelated;Images may not cover the service boundary.\nA SaaS team periodically runs broader checks after focused selections. Why?|Challenge selection gaps|Make all focused tests useless|Prove omitted routes forever|Replace impact reasoning|Broader checkpoints can expose assumptions missed by a narrower impact model.|Focused checks retain value;A broad run is still finite;Reasoning remains necessary.\nA university incident reveals an unmodeled export dependency. What should happen?|Update mappings and evidence|Hide the escaped failure|Keep the map unchanged|Declare all tools invalid|The new relationship should inform future selection and appropriate regression cases.|Concealment prevents learning;The model has new evidence;One gap does not invalidate every tool."
